**New Evidence**
- Scholz’s government framed the new Bürgergeld as more security, more respect, and more freedom, with state help meant to be closer, less bureaucratic, and more targeted. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Das neue Bürgergeld: Mehr Respekt und Sicherheit, weniger Bürokratie","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-07-20","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Presse/Meldungen/2022/das-neue-buergergeld-mehr-respekt-und-sicherheit-weniger-buerokratie.html","claim":"The new Bürgergeld was described as more security, more respect, and more freedom, and the state help was to become more citizen-proximate, less bureaucratic, and more targeted.","locator":"lines 792-796","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry text announcing a flagship social-reform design under the Scholz cabinet.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 social-policy intent and design.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Scholz coalition government through BMAS; this is policy intent rather than direct personal implementation.","contrary_evidence":["This is a reform proposal, not delivery evidence by itself."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.4","6B.5","6B.7","6B.8","6B.9"]}

- The Bürgergeld law was adopted in 2022, took effect on January 1, 2023, and explicitly updated working-age basic security. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bürgergeld-Gesetz","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-12-20","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Gesetze-und-Gesetzesvorhaben/buergergeld-gesetz.html","claim":"The Bürgergeld law was enacted in 2022, took effect on January 1, 2023, and is presented as bringing the working-age basic-security system up to date.","locator":"lines 790-804, 818-825","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry law page with enactment status and implementation timetable.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 enactment for a core social reform tied to Scholz's first year.","ruler_attribution":"Coalition legislation under Scholz; institutional implementation was shared with the Bundestag, Bundesrat, and jobcenters.","contrary_evidence":["Because implementation began in 2023, it is only partly visible inside the 2022 period."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The government created a child immediate supplement and a one-off payment to support low-income families, with 2.9 million children targeted and a July 2022 payout. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mehr Unterstützung für Familien mit wenig Geld","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-03-16","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Presse/Pressemitteilungen/2022/mehr-unterstuetzung-fuer-familien-mit-wenig-geld.html","claim":"The cabinet approved a 20-euro monthly child immediate supplement and a 100-euro one-off payment for people receiving social minimum-security benefits, targeting about 2.9 million children and a July 2022 payout.","locator":"lines 797-805","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry press release with direct beneficiary counts and implementation timing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 crisis-response social policy.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet action under Scholz; the measure is institutional rather than personal, but clearly attributable to his government.","contrary_evidence":["The measure is temporary and does not by itself solve child poverty."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- The Bundestag approved the government bill to raise the minimum wage to 12 euros per hour from October 1, 2022, benefiting more than six million people, especially women and workers in eastern Germany. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Der Mindestlohn steigt auf 12 Euro","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-06-03","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Presse/Meldungen/2022/mindestlohn-steigt-auf-12-euro.html","claim":"The Bundestag approved the government bill to raise the statutory minimum wage to 12 euros per hour from October 1, 2022, benefiting over six million people, especially women and workers in eastern Germany.","locator":"lines 794-797","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry release on a passed federal bill with clear beneficiary scale and timing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 wage and household-income policy.","ruler_attribution":"Coalition policy under Scholz and parliament; not a personal administrative action, but clearly a government choice.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation still eroded purchasing power in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- The housing ministry said Wohngeld Plus was the biggest housing-benefit reform in the program’s history and would support 4.5 million people in two million households. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mehr Geld für die Wohnkosten im Winter","publisher":"BMWSB","publication_date":"2022-09-28","url":"https://www.bmwsb.bund.de/SharedDocs/pressemitteilungen/DE/2022/09/wohngeld-plus.html","claim":"Wohngeld Plus was described as the largest reform since the housing benefit began and was projected to support 4.5 million people in two million households, with about 190 euros more per month on average.","locator":"lines 277-285","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry press release with concrete scale, timing, and beneficiary groups.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 housing-policy decision, with implementation beginning in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet policy under Scholz; implementation is shared with Länder and local housing offices.","contrary_evidence":["Most of the cash effect begins in 2023, so the 2022 evidence is mostly policy design and approval."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The Bundesrat-approved Wohngeld Plus reform was set to begin on January 1, 2023, with up to two million households eligible and average support rising from 180 to 370 euros per month. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Das Wohngeld-Plus kommt!","publisher":"BMWSB","publication_date":"2022-11-25","url":"https://www.bmwsb.bund.de/SharedDocs/pressemitteilungen/DE/2022/11/wohngeld-bundesrat.html","claim":"The Bundesrat approved the reform, making up to two million households eligible from January 1, 2023, with average monthly support rising from 180 to 370 euros and heating costs permanently subsidized.","locator":"lines 276-279","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry release on the final legislative step and beneficiary effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 enactment for a 2023 social-protection expansion.","ruler_attribution":"Government policy under Scholz, carried through the federal legislative process and Länder implementation.","contrary_evidence":["This is not a 2022 delivery outcome; the effect is mainly forward-looking."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The federal government moved Ukrainian refugees into basic social-security coverage from June 1, 2022, with jobcenter access, integration and language courses, labor-market support, childcare support, and health coverage. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Couverture de base pour les réfugiés ukrainiens","publisher":"Bundesregierung","publication_date":"2022-04-27","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-fr/service/archives/couverture-de-base-ukrainiens-2028896","claim":"Registered Ukrainian refugees were to receive expanded basic social-security coverage from June 1, 2022, with jobcenter access, integration and language courses, labor-market support, childcare support, and health coverage.","locator":"lines 141-168","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary federal-government archive page with explicit cabinet decision and service details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 refugee-protection and welfare policy.","ruler_attribution":"Federal cabinet action under Scholz; delivery shared with Länder and local jobcenters.","contrary_evidence":["This is a policy decision, not a full outcomes evaluation for integration or service quality."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- The finance ministry said the Bund helped municipalities with extra funds in 2022, especially for refugee financing and public transport, and that the energy-crisis packages should stabilize local finances. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bundespolitik und Kommunalfinanzen in Krisenzeiten","publisher":"Bundesministerium der Finanzen","publication_date":"2022-11","url":"https://www.bundesfinanzministerium.de/Monatsberichte/2022/11/Inhalte/Kapitel-3-Analysen/3-1-bundespolitik-und-kommunalfinanzen-in-krisenzeiten.html","claim":"The federal government supported municipalities with additional funds in 2022, especially for refugee financing and public transport, and the energy-crisis packages were expected to stabilize municipal finances.","locator":"lines 138-145, 228-231","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary finance-ministry analysis of municipal finance and crisis support; slightly less direct than a press release but still official and specific.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 fiscal and administrative crisis response.","ruler_attribution":"Federal-government support under Scholz; implementation and effects are shared with Länder and municipalities.","contrary_evidence":["The same text notes that municipal finances were under pressure from current crises and future deficits."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.6","6B.9"]}

- The OECD said Germany acted swiftly to secure energy supply and support households and firms, but that the support was not well targeted to vulnerable households and highly exposed firms. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: Germany 2023","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-germany-2023_9642a3f5-en/full-report/component-4.html","claim":"The OECD said the government acted swiftly to support households and firms and secure energy supply, while noting that some support measures were not well targeted to vulnerable households and highly exposed firms.","locator":"lines 3928-3944","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral assessment with detailed policy description and an explicit critique of targeting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retroactive assessment of the 2022 response and its immediate design.","ruler_attribution":"External evaluation of the Scholz government's policy package, not a direct measure of personal ministerial management.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD explicitly criticized the targeting quality of the support."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The IMF said Germany expanded income support for vulnerable households, cut fuel taxes, and provided liquidity support to firms; it also commended the authorities’ timely and well-designed response to the Ukraine spillovers. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with Germany","publisher":"IMF","publication_date":"2022-07-19","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/07/19/pr22268-germany-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-germany","claim":"The IMF said Germany was expanding income support for vulnerable households, cutting fuel taxes, and providing liquidity support to firms, and commended the authorities' timely and well-designed response to the Ukraine spillovers.","locator":"lines 243-252","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral assessment with concrete policy content and evaluative judgment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy assessment during the shock year.","ruler_attribution":"External appraisal of the Scholz government's response; it does not prove personal control over every implementing instrument.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF also urged more targeted and time-bound support for vulnerable households."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- Destatis reported that consumer prices rose 7.9 percent in 2022, mainly because of energy and food shocks after the Ukraine war, though relief measures partly mitigated the increase. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Inflation rate at +7.9% in 2022","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-01-17","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/01/PE23_022_611.html","claim":"Germany's consumer prices rose 7.9 percent in 2022, mainly due to energy and food price spikes after the war in Ukraine, and relief measures partly mitigated the increase.","locator":"press-release annual-average section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statistics with exact annual figures and explanatory context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data published immediately after year-end.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context under Scholz's government rather than a clean ruler-caused effect; useful as a constraint on welfare performance.","contrary_evidence":["The same release shows households still faced very large price increases despite mitigation."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}

- Destatis said real earnings in Germany fell 4.0 percent in 2022 compared with 2021, the largest loss since 2008. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"After revision: real earnings down 4.0% in 2022 from 2021","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-04-27","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/04/PE23_166_62321.html","claim":"Real earnings in Germany decreased by an average 4.0 percent in 2022 compared with 2021, the largest loss since 2008.","locator":"press release body","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statistics release with revised annual estimate and clear year-on-year result.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data, albeit published with later revision.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context under Scholz; the loss reflects inflation shock as much as policy choices.","contrary_evidence":["This is negative welfare evidence and shows that price shocks overwhelmed nominal earnings gains."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}

- Destatis said 20.9 percent of Germany’s population, about 17.3 million people, were at risk of poverty or social exclusion in 2022, almost unchanged from 2021. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Just over one-fifth of Germany’s population at risk of poverty or social exclusion","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-05-16","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/05/PE23_190_63.html","claim":"Just over 17.3 million people in Germany were affected by poverty or social exclusion in 2022, equal to 20.9 percent of the population, and the value was almost unchanged from 2021.","locator":"press release summary and opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statistics release with explicit annual poverty-risk figures and year-on-year comparison.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data published in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context under Scholz; it constrains claims of broad human-development improvement.","contrary_evidence":["The indicator did not materially improve year on year."],"lenses":["6B.8","6B.10"]}

- Destatis said employment in Germany in 2022 reached about 45.6 million people, the highest level since reunification, up by 589,000 from 2021. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Employment in 2022 at highest level since German unification","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-01-02","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/01/PE23_001_13321.html","claim":"Average employment in 2022 reached about 45.6 million people, the highest level since German reunification, rising by 589,000 from 2021.","locator":"press release opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statistics release with clear headline figure and year-on-year change.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 labor-market outcome data.","ruler_attribution":"Macro outcome during Scholz's first full year; still partly inherited and affected by migration and preexisting labor-market strength.","contrary_evidence":["Employment strength did not offset the simultaneous erosion in real earnings and persistent poverty risk."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I reviewed roughly two dozen distinct documents across BMAS, BMWSB, BMF, Bundesregierung, Destatis, OECD, and IMF material, then kept the strongest non-duplicative items.
- I rejected duplicate or near-duplicate pages that repeated the same 2022 policy without adding a materially distinct claim, especially alternate-language mirrors and follow-up pages on the same relief measures.
- I excluded later 2023-2024 follow-up pages when they only repeated the same programs, because they did not add target-period 2022 evidence beyond the pages above.
- I treated generic landing pages, ministry navigation pages, and broad topical pages as weak unless they contained a specific 2022 claim with a usable locator.
- I also excluded baseline-only welfare context that did not show a ruler-linked 2022 decision or a clear 2022 outcome relevant to this chapter.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence on 6B.4 is still thinner than the policy-output evidence. The dossier shows major social spending and crisis administration, but there is not much direct inspection of whether the relevant federal and local social-service machinery was staffed or managed especially well.
- 6B.9 is only partially covered. Bürgergeld and Wohngeld Plus point toward durable institutional reform, but most effects were scheduled for 2023, so the 2022 record is stronger on design than on proven durability.
- 6B.3 is reasonably covered for low-income households, children, women, pensioners, and Ukrainian refugees, but weaker for disabled people and some minority subgroups.
- 6B.5 is covered mainly by OECD/IMF assessments plus the design of the reforms. More direct evidence on measurement, correction, and administrative feedback loops would help.
- If you want, I can do one more pass focused only on 6B.4, 6B.5, and 6B.9.
